'''Mizuno Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|水野県}}, ''Mizuno-ken'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Nankai]] Region in the south-central highlands of the island of [[Yagikoshu]]. With a population of 581,728 as of 2026 spread across 9,144 km², it is a sparsely populated, mountainous prefecture known for its precision instruments industry, its lacquered woodworking, and its association with the [[Honda clan]]—the imperial family claims descent from the semi-legendary [[Emperor Mizuno]].

Mizuno Prefecture is Okaiken's centre of precision manufacturing, producing watches, optical instruments, and scientific equipment that are exported across the [[East Fosian Sphere]]. The prefecture is also known for the {{nihongo|Kakunodate|角館}} samurai district, one of the best-preserved historical streetscapes in Yagikoshu, and for its {{nihongo|''kaba-zaiku''|樺細工}}—cherry-bark lacquerware, a craft unique to the prefecture.

== History ==

=== The imperial connection ===
[[File:Kakunodate Samurai District.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Kakunodate Samurai District, one of the best-preserved historical streetscapes in Yagikoshu]]
The Honda clan's claim to imperial descent runs through the semi-legendary [[Emperor Mizuno]], said to have ruled from a palace in the [[Suwa]] basin in the 3rd century AD. While the historical existence of Emperor Mizuno is debated, the association has given the prefecture a special status in Sanese imperial mythology, and the {{nihongo|Mizuno Shrine|水野神宮|Mizuno Jingū}} is one of the most important Shinto shrines in southern Yagikoshu.

During the [[Honda Shogunate]], the Mizuno basin was governed by a cadet branch of the Honda family, who developed the region's forestry and lacquerware traditions. The {{nihongo|Kakunodate Samurai District|角館武家屋敷|Kakunodate Bukeyashiki}}, built in 1623, survives as a remarkably intact example of shogunal-era urban planning.

=== The precision revolution ===
Mizuno's transformation from a remote mountain prefecture to a centre of precision manufacturing began in 1911, when the {{nihongo|Mizuno Watch Company|水野時計| Mizuno Tokei}} was founded in the city of Mizuno. The company's founder, {{nihongo|Takeda Ginji|武田銀次}}, recognised that the prefecture's clean mountain air, abundant hydroelectric power, and tradition of meticulous craftsmanship made it an ideal location for watchmaking.

The Mizuno Watch Company grew into one of Okaiken's largest precision manufacturers, producing timepieces, optical instruments, and scientific equipment. The company's {{nihongo|''Mizuno Chronometer''|水野クロノメーター|Mizuno Kuronomētā}}, used by the Sanese Imperial Navy for navigation, was considered the most accurate mechanical timepiece produced in Adonia during the 1930s and 1940s.

== Geography ==

Mizuno Prefecture occupies the south-central highlands of Yagikoshu, with no coastline. The prefecture is bounded by [[Nagato Prefecture]] to the east, [[Uruwa Prefecture]] to the south, and the mountainous interior to the north and west. The prefecture's geography is defined by the [[Mizuno Basin]]—a large, elevated intermontane valley at approximately 700 metres above sea level—surrounded by forested mountains rising to over 2,000 metres.

The [[Suwa]] area in the northern part of the prefecture contains several large lakes, formed by volcanic activity, that provide freshwater and hydroelectric power. The [[Kawagoe]] River drains the southern highlands.

=== Climate ===
Mizuno Prefecture has an [[w:Oceanic climate|oceanic climate]] (''Cfb'') at the basin elevation, with warm summers and cold, snowy winters. The elevated position means winter temperatures regularly fall below −10°C, and the basin receives heavy snowfall—over 4 metres annually in some areas. Summers are pleasant, with temperatures typically 24–28°C, making the prefecture a popular summer retreat from the coastal heat.

