== Economy ==

Mizuno Prefecture's economy is driven by precision instruments, cherry-bark lacquerware, and hydroelectric power. The prefecture's GDP was approximately 円22 billion in 2025, with industry accounting for 45%, services 38%, and agriculture 17%.

=== Precision instruments ===
[[File:Mizuno Precision Corridor.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Mizuno Precision Corridor, centre of Okaiken's precision manufacturing industry]]
The {{nihongo|Mizuno Precision Corridor|水野精密回廊|Mizuno Seimitsu Kairō}}, stretching from the city of Mizuno to [[Suwa]], houses approximately 80 firms producing watches, optical instruments, scientific equipment, and semiconductor lithography components. The corridor is the centre of Okaiken's precision manufacturing industry, employing approximately 18,000 workers and generating approximately 円12 billion in annual revenue.

The largest employer is [[Mizuno Precision Industries]] (formerly the Mizuno Watch Company), which produces mechanical and quartz timepieces, optical lenses for medical and scientific instruments, and precision-machined components for the semiconductor industry. The company's {{nihongo|''Mizuno Astron''|水野アストロン|Mizuno Asutoron}} line of solar-powered quartz watches is one of the best-selling watch brands in the [[East Fosian Sphere]].

=== Cherry-bark lacquerware ===
[[File:Kaba-zaiku Cherry Bark.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Cherry-bark lacquerware (kaba-zaiku), a craft unique to Mizuno Prefecture]]
The {{nihongo|''kaba-zaiku''|樺細工}}—cherry-bark lacquerware—is a craft unique to Mizuno Prefecture, practised in the [[Kakunodate]] district for over 400 years. The technique involves peeling the bark from wild cherry trees in a way that allows the tree to regenerate, then polishing and lacquering the bark to create a smooth, lustrous surface that is applied to boxes, containers, and decorative objects. The craft is designated a [[National Intangible Cultural Property]], and approximately 50 artisans practise it today, producing pieces that command prices of 円50,000–円500,000.

=== Hydroelectric power ===
The prefecture's abundant rainfall and mountainous terrain make it one of Okaiken's most important hydroelectric regions. The {{nihongo|Suwa Hydroelectric Complex|諏訪水力発電所|Suwa Suiryoku Hatsudensho}} operates 8 dams and 14 generating stations, producing approximately 2.4 GW of electricity—roughly 15% of Yagikoshu's total hydroelectric output. The surplus power is exported to the [[Toyozawa Data Corridor]] and the [[Obama Data Centre]] via the national grid.
Mizuno's economy is centered on high-precision optical instruments and exquisite forest craft. Specialized cleanroom laboratories manufacture multi-element camera lenses, subsea endoscopic inspection tools, and laser alignment optics for medical and marine engineering applications. Complementing this high-tech manufacturing, local artisans preserve the unique kabazaiku craft, polishing wild cherry bark to veneer durable, moisture-resistant tea caddies and decorative stationery boxes.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Okaiken Railways]] [[Mizuno Line]] connects [[Mizuno Station]] to [[Toyozawa Central Station]] in approximately 160 minutes on express services, via the [[Nankai Tunnel]].

=== Air ===
[[Mizuno Airport]] (IATA: MZO) operates domestic flights to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]].

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Mizuno's mountain cuisine is hearty and warming. The prefecture's signature dish is {{nihongo|''Mizuno oyaki''|水野おやき}}—stuffed dumplings made from buckwheat or wheat flour dough, filled with vegetables, miso, or sweet bean paste, and grilled on an iron plate. Other specialities include {{nihongo|''suwa masu-zushi''|諏訪鱒寿司}} (trout sushi from Lake Suwa), {{nihongo|''shinshu miso''|信州味噌}} (a dark, rich miso paste), and {{nihongo|''wasabi''|山葵}}—fresh wasabi, cultivated in the prefecture's cold, clean mountain streams.

=== Festivals ===
* {{nihongo|'''Kakunodate Cherry Bark Festival'''|角館樺祭り|Kakunodate Kaba Matsuri}} — Annual lacquerware exhibition (May)
* {{nihongo|'''Mizuno Time Festival'''|水野時祭り|Mizuno Toki Matsuri}} — Annual celebration of the precision industry (October)
* {{nihongo|'''Suwa Lake Festival'''|諏訪湖祭り|Suwa-ko Matsuri}} — Summer fireworks and lantern festival (January)

